{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "SMRITI: NER Problem Statement Gap Tour",
  "description": "Checks SMRITI against the NER dementia problem statement (a-h + expected solution) and walks each place a feature is missing or only partial. Already covered: 15 games, adaptive difficulty, 4 reminder types, caregiver dashboard, PWA offline sync, encrypted local data, voice input, family message board.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/lib/i18n/languages.ts",
      "line": 13,
      "title": "1. Language coverage: only 7 of NER's languages",
      "description": "LANGUAGES = as, hi, en, brx, mni, bn, ne. Six NER languages (Khasi, Garo, Mizo, Kokborok, Bhutia, Lepcha) were probed against Bhashini and found unsupported, so they are deliberately absent. Gap vs (c): Meghalaya, Mizoram, Tripura, Sikkim and Nagaland patients get no native UI. Note types.ts already reserves 'kha' and 'lus' for patients only. Gotcha: fixing this needs a non-Bhashini path (recorded human voice packs, community translation), not just a locale JSON."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/lib/i18n/locales/mni.json",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "2. Manipuri and Bodo UI are only 36% translated",
      "description": "mni.json and brx.json hold 66 of en.json's 183 keys; as, hi, bn, ne are 183/183. Missing keys fall back to English, so a Bodo or Manipuri elder sees mixed-language screens, and the game and reminder text is the part most likely to be in English. Gap vs (c) and (d). Gotcha: Manipuri is rendered in Bengali script here (see languages.ts NATIVE_LANGUAGE_NAME), not Meitei Mayek, which is the script Manipuri families actually read."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/lib/engine/objects.ts",
      "line": 32,
      "title": "3. Cultural object content is Assamese-centric",
      "description": "OBJECTS has good NE items (gamosa, jaapi, xorai, rhino) but each name only carries en/as/hi; bn, ne, mni, brx fall back to English via objectName(). Nothing represents Meghalaya, Manipur, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim or Tripura daily life (Khasi jymphong, Naga shawl, Mizo puanchei, Manipuri phanek). Gap vs (d): 'culturally familiar themes' is delivered for one state of eight. No bhajan, folk-song or festival audio exists anywhere in src or games."
    },
    {
      "directory": "smriti/public/audio",
      "title": "4. Bundled voice audio is empty: offline voice depends on device or Bhashini",
      "description": "public/audio/{as,en,hi} contain only .gitkeep, and audio/prompts.ts is literally 'export {}'. next.config.js line 32 caches /audio/* CacheFirst, but there is nothing to cache. Spoken prompts therefore come from the browser's Web Speech voices or online Bhashini TTS. Gap vs (c)+(g): on a cheap Android tablet with no Assamese/Bodo/Manipuri voice installed, the app stays silent offline (speech.ts says so explicitly). Fix: pre-recorded or pre-synthesised packs for reminders and game instructions, shipped in the precache."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/lib/audio/speech.ts",
      "line": 36,
      "title": "5. speak() stays silent when no matching voice exists",
      "description": "speak() picks an installed voice for the language and no-ops otherwise. Bodo and Manipuri are mapped to ISO codes that almost no device voice matches. The same file's comment admits this. Implication: the 'voice-enabled multilingual interface' requirement is only met for languages Bhashini serves online, and voice input (voiceInput.ts) has no ASR for Bodo, Manipuri or Nepali at all."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/hooks/useReminders.ts",
      "line": 63,
      "title": "6. Reminders are foreground-only, no real push",
      "description": "Reminders poll on an interval and fire `new Notification(...)` only while the page is open and permission was granted. There is no service-worker push, no scheduled local notification, no web-push dependency in package.json. Gap vs (e): if the tablet screen is locked or the app is closed, the medicine reminder never fires. Gotcha: the caregiver can also not be told when a dose was missed, see next steps."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/app/api/sync/route.ts",
      "line": 291,
      "title": "7. Caregiver alerts are database rows only, never delivered",
      "description": "Cognitive-drop, missed-sessions and low-adherence alerts are inserted into the alerts table (also lines 320-370) and read back by GET /api/alerts. No SMS, WhatsApp, email or push is sent anywhere (grep for twilio/web-push/sms finds nothing). Gap vs 'caregiver monitoring and alert system': a caregiver only learns of a red alert by opening the dashboard. Remote families and ASHA workers are the exact users who will not."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/lib/supabase/types.ts",
      "line": 71,
      "title": "8. One caregiver per patient, no care team",
      "description": "Patient.caregiver_id is a single foreign key, although CaregiverRole already lists 'asha_worker', 'nurse' and 'clinician'. A family member and an ASHA worker cannot both monitor the same patient, and alerts (sync route line 293) go to that one owner. Gap vs (f) 'caregivers and healthcare workers': roles exist as labels, but there is no shared-care model, no worker-level view across households, and family-share links are read-only summaries."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/lib/engine/alerts.ts",
      "line": 6,
      "title": "9. Alert rules are thin: no clinical or safety triggers",
      "description": "Only three detectors exist: detectCognitiveDrop (2-stddev), detectMissedSessions (3 days), detectLowAdherence (<50%). Nothing for wandering, falls, an SOS or panic button on the patient screen, night-time activity, or repeated distress in companion chat beyond distress-keywords.ts. Gap vs 'caregiver monitoring and alert system' and 'reduce anxiety and confusion': no emergency path from the elder to the caregiver."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/lib/games/difficulty-ml.ts",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "10. Adaptive AI exists but is bootstrapped, not clinically validated",
      "description": "A 20-parameter logistic regression trained on ~10,000 synthetic sessions whose labels are the old rule engine's decisions. It is honest about that in the header. Gap vs (b): no real patient data loop, no per-patient personalisation or retraining, and no baseline cognitive screening (only MoCA-inspired game design, no actual screening flow) to support 'early cognitive intervention'. The dashboard score is an activity-based estimate, not an assessment."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/app/caregiver/settings/page.tsx",
      "line": 53,
      "title": "11. Data control: delete-all exists, export and consent do not",
      "description": "Settings offers a PIN-gated 'delete all data' flow and device-side encryption is in place. Missing for 'secure patient data management': no data export (no CSV, PDF or print of progress for a doctor visit), no consent record for a patient with dementia or their family, no audit log of who viewed what, and no privacy-policy or DPDP-style notice flow. Clinicians in remote areas cannot receive a report, only a share-link summary."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/next.config.js",
      "line": 41,
      "title": "12. Offline works for the shell, sync is online-only and unverified at scale",
      "description": "next-pwa caches pages, audio and images; /api/sync is NetworkOnly and Dexie holds local data, so play and reminders work offline (good). Gaps vs (g): no background sync (no service-worker sync event), so data only uploads when the app is reopened online; no conflict-resolution UI; and no offline-first content bundle for the non-English languages (see step 4). Remote-area tablets that stay offline for weeks will show stale caregiver dashboards."
    },
    {
      "file": "smriti/src/components/patient/FamilyMessageBoard.tsx",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "13. Social interaction is one-way notes, nothing live or communal",
      "description": "Family can leave short notes and the elder can hear them, and that is the whole social layer. Missing vs 'social interaction among elderly users': no voice or video message from family, no call button (no tel: or WebRTC anywhere), no group or community games, no peer or leaderboard for elders, and no scheduled family game session. Isolation is a named problem in the statement."
    },
    {
      "title": "Next steps: rough priority",
      "description": "Highest impact for the stated users first: (1) real caregiver alert delivery (SMS or WhatsApp) plus a shared care team for ASHA workers, steps 7-8; (2) bundled offline voice packs and finishing Bodo/Manipuri strings, steps 2, 4, 5; (3) background push reminders, step 6; (4) patient SOS button and clinician export, steps 9 and 11; (5) more NE regional content and languages, steps 1 and 3; (6) family voice/video and a baseline screening flow, steps 10 and 13. Already strong and not listed above: 15 games across memory, attention, routine recall and recognition, four reminder types with appointment prompts, PWA offline, encrypted local storage, elder-friendly sizing."
    }
  ]
}
